toJSON -- encode Macaulay2 things as JSON data
**********************************************

  * Usage: 
        toJSON x
  * Inputs:
      * x, a *note thing: (Macaulay2Doc)Thing,, 
  * *note Optional inputs: (Macaulay2Doc)using functions with optional inputs,:
      * Indent => *note null: (Macaulay2Doc)null,, an *note integer:
        (Macaulay2Doc)ZZ,, or a *note string: (Macaulay2Doc)String,, default
        value null, how much to indent;
      * IndentLevel => an *note integer: (Macaulay2Doc)ZZ,, default value 0,
        current indentation level (intended for internal use);
      * ValueSeparator => *note null: (Macaulay2Doc)null, or a *note string:
        (Macaulay2Doc)String,, default value null, the string between values;
      * NameSeparator => a *note string: (Macaulay2Doc)String,, default value
        ": ", the string after names in object members;
      * Sort => a *note Boolean value: (Macaulay2Doc)Boolean,, default value
        false, whether to sort object members;
  * Outputs:
      * a *note string: (Macaulay2Doc)String,, containing JSON data; 

Description
===========

This method (which is also available using its synonym json) returns a string
containing JSON data corresponding to the given Macaulay2 thing.  If the Indent
option is null (the default), then there are no newlines or indentation.

+-------------------------------------------------------------+
|i1 : x = hashTable {"foo" => {1, 2, {pi, true, false, null}}}|
|                                                             |
|o1 = HashTable{"foo" => {1, 2, {pi, true, false, }}}         |
|                                                             |
|o1 : HashTable                                               |
+-------------------------------------------------------------+
|i2 : toJSON x                                                |
|                                                             |
|o2 = {"foo": [1, 2, [3.141592653589793, true, false, null]]} |
+-------------------------------------------------------------+

If the Indent option is an integer, then newlines are added between values of
arrays and members of lists and the given integer determines the number of
spaces to indent for each level of indentation.

+-----------------------------+
|i3 : toJSON(x, Indent => 2)  |
|                             |
|o3 = {                       |
|       "foo": [              |
|         1,                  |
|         2,                  |
|         [                   |
|           3.141592653589793,|
|           true,             |
|           false,            |
|           null              |
|         ]                   |
|       ]                     |
|     }                       |
+-----------------------------+

Alternatively, the Indent option can be a string corresponding to the
indentation used for each level.

+-----------------------------------------------+
|i4 : toJSON(x, Indent => "\t")                 |
|                                               |
|o4 = {                                         |
|             "foo": [                          |
|                     1,                        |
|                     2,                        |
|                     [                         |
|                             3.141592653589793,|
|                             true,             |
|                             false,            |
|                             null              |
|                     ]                         |
|             ]                                 |
|     }                                         |
+-----------------------------------------------+

The ValueSeparator option determines the string to use between values in an
array and members in an object.  If it is null (the default), then the string
will either be ", " or ",", depending on whether Indent is null or not,
respectively. Otherwise, the given string is used.

+-----------------------------------------------------------------+
|i5 : toJSON(x, ValueSeparator => " , ")                          |
|                                                                 |
|o5 = {"foo": [1 , 2 , [3.141592653589793 , true , false , null]]}|
+-----------------------------------------------------------------+

The NameSeparator option determines the string to use after the name of an
object member.  By default, it is ": ".

+-------------------------------------------------------------+
|i6 : toJSON(x, NameSeparator => " : ")                       |
|                                                             |
|o6 = {"foo" : [1, 2, [3.141592653589793, true, false, null]]}|
+-------------------------------------------------------------+

By default, the members of objects are given in arbitrary order.  To sort them
by name in lexicographic order as strings, use the Sort option.

+------------------------------------------------------------------------+
|i7 : toJSON(hashTable{"foo" => 1, "bar" => 2, "baz" => 3}, Sort => true)|
|                                                                        |
|o7 = {"bar": 2, "baz": 3, "foo": 1}                                     |
+------------------------------------------------------------------------+

